WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.480
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: So may I move us now please on to item 8 on the agenda, which is the

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:16.440
corporate and performance report.  Matthew, can i ask you to kick off please.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:25.080
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Let me first follow Simon's
words by just paying tribute to the NHS emergency

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:31.169
services staff who responded with their usual
professionalism last Wednesday, and particularly

00:00:31.169 --> 00:00:36.230
to the ambulance and paramedic staff, the
staff from St Thomas' who came onto the bridge

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:40.539
to care for injured people, and to those at
St Thomas', King's, Chelsea and Westminster,

00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:47.120
St Mary's and the Royal London who took patients
from the incident. I think it was another

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.650
example of the NHS at its best.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:01:03.620
Looking at the performance for January, A&amp;E
attendances in the last three months have

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:10.410
fallen to a growth rate of 1.7%; so over the
year, they are up by 3.3%, but over the last

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:18.430
three months, that rate has been 1.7%. They
are actually 1% lower than they were in January

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:24.270
2016, so that is the first time since April
2016 that we have had a month-on-month fall.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:32.370
Emergency admissions were up by 1.5% in January
2016, up 3.3% over the past 12 months. I think

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:38.810
there is some good news in there, that we
are seeing demand growth moderate as the urgent

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:43.290
and emergency care strategy starts to have
an effect, as primary and community services

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:49.040
are going the extra mile, and as the public
are working with us to access the best services

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:57.680
for their needs. Overall, A&amp;E performance
against the four-hour wait standard fell to

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:04.110
85.1% of people being discharged and treated
within four hours, which is not an acceptable

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:11.310
position. Of the third of those people who
attended a minor injuries unit, a walk-in

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:19.370
centre or an urgent treatment centre, 99.4%
were treated within four hours, so it shows

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:24.700
that it is both to the benefit of patients
and the NHS as a whole for people to use the

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.660
right facilities and use urgent treatment
centres when they can, which is why in the

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:38.220
Urgent and Emergency Care Plan for next year
we will be enhancing 111, we will be developing

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:45.389
urgent treatment centres, and we will be introducing
streaming within A&amp;E departments to ensure

00:02:45.389 --> 00:02:48.840
that people get to the right care.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:55.040
In terms of delayed transfers of care, there
was a small increase in the bed days lost

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:03.500
to delayed transfers of care in January to
197,000 bed days. That is the equivalent of

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:15.950
an average of 6,356 beds lost each day from
appropriate NHS use; that is up 23% on the

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:23.690
previous 12 months, so this is a significant
issue. We have seen a 191,000-bed-day increase

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:32.440
in the NHS delays - that is a 17.5% increase
- and a 209,000 increase in the bed days lost

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:40.371
to social care delays; that is a 39% increase.
In the coming year, there is going to be a

00:03:40.371 --> 00:03:46.459
very significant focus on the NHS improving
its discharge management and reducing its

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:52.370
delayed transfers of care, and it is really
crucial that the additional funding from the

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:58.160
Budget that has gone into social care is used,
in part, to ensure that patients who are stuck

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:05.120
in hospital due to an absence of home care
or care home places get access to those services.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:12.870
Because we know it is important in a whole
host of areas: it is important for patients

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:18.260
themselves; these are usually elderly people
who are having their liberty constrained,

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:26.060
and spending too long in hospital rather than
being in the most appropriate place for them

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:32.530
to enjoy their older years. It is important
for patients who are in A&amp;E, because one of

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:36.330
the major reasons why people are not getting
through A&amp;E and admitted to the bed that need

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:40.830
in hospital is because that bed is filled
by somebody who should be being cared for

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:45.500
somewhere else. It is important for people
who are waiting for surgery, because we are

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:51.220
cancelling operations because there is not
a bed to put that person into. It is important

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:56.820
for the finances of the NHS, because trying
to run a hospital at 95% bed occupancy or

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:02.340
higher is not an efficient way of running
the services, so on all counts this is a really

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:06.370
high priority for us next year.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:17.280
111 answered 1.27 million calls in January:
that is 45,000 more than January 2016, and

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:25.140
over 88% of those were answered within 60
seconds, which is better than December, the

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:31.350
month before, and better than January the
year before.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:35.481
As we go forward, we are trying to put in
place the set of actions around urgent and

00:05:35.481 --> 00:05:40.140
emergency care which will turn the corner
on performance and see that going back up

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:46.230
to the standards that we expect, and it will
be a big focus for next year. Clearly, freeing

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:52.250
up bed capacity, both social care discharges
and NHS discharges, to try and get the 2,000

00:05:52.250 --> 00:06:01.250
to 3,000 beds back into use that currently
have social care patients in them; managing

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:09.490
demand, so standardising urgent treatment
centres so that they are uniformly consistent

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:14.639
in the high-quality care that they can offer
and services they have; supporting care homes

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:21.320
to manage patients better, so we get fewer
patients admitted to hospital from care homes;

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:26.080
increasing the clinical support within 111,
so that when you ring there, your chance of

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:31.110
getting to speak to an appropriate clinician
and therefore avoiding having to go to a GP

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:36.290
or avoiding having to go to A&amp;E, will go up;
and the front-door streaming that I talked

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:44.490
about, will all be targeted at recovering
our A&amp;E performance.

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:53.810
In other areas, we have seen the referral
to treatment has been at about 90% for the

00:06:53.810 --> 00:07:00.780
past month, of people seen and treated within
18 weeks. That is a small improvement on December.

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:07.600
We have seen cancer hitting four out of five
of the cancer standards, so the focus in the

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:11.180
coming year will be on getting the 62-day
standard back on track to where it needs to

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:18.960
be. We have seen all three standards for access
to psychological therapies continue being

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:25.620
hit. I think there is some really strong performance
in there, but we really need to get our focus

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.850
on the emergency care, the right people in
hospitals, and getting flow-back into the

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:33.400
system so that people get the right care in
the right place.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:38.000
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Matthew. I think
it is conceivable that the Delivery Plan tomorrow

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:41.900
may have some things to say on those critical
areas.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:51.310
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Thank you, Matthew. Local
authorities have been experiencing quite significant

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:56.570
financial pressures. How do you plan to work
with local authorities to ensure that the

00:07:56.570 --> 00:08:03.160
funding on social care really benefits citizens,
as you say, who are inappropriately delayed

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:04.160
in hospitals?

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:11.350
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: There is no question
that local authorities generally, and social

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:15.449
care specifically, have come under huge financial
pressure over the past few years, which is

00:08:15.449 --> 00:08:21.360
why we have made the argument consistently
over the past year for additional investment

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:27.820
in social care, above and beyond additional
investment in the NHS, so we are delighted

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:37.580
to see the announcement in the Budget. The
caveat that the Chancellor put around that

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:44.169
in his speech was that councils need to take
immediate action to fund care packages for

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:49.760
more people, support social care providers
and relieve pressure on the NHS locally, and

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:55.190
I think that plays well into the partnership
work that has been going on through STPs over

00:08:55.190 --> 00:09:01.490
the past year, where increasingly, health
and social care are working very closely together

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:06.320
to address these issues, and it is important
now that that goodwill and that close working

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.120
now turns into real action for patients.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:20.000
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Matthew, I was concerned by
the number of 111 calls that resulted in an

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.700
ambulance being dispatched, the 13%, and that
did seem to me to be exceptionally high; the

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:29.950
idea that one person in six who rings 111
is going to end up in an ambulance, with obvious

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:35.620
knock-on effects for other patients who need
ambulances, and for the congestion that we

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:41.400
are seeing in A&amp;E departments. I wondered
if you might tell us a bit more about what

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:47.770
you are doing to support 111, both in terms
of increasing the clinical expertise in 111

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:54.620
but also, I think at least as importantly,
to refine the algorithms using that experience

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:57.570
base of 1.3 million calls per month?

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:04.790
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: It is a very good question,
and I think over the next few years, 111 will

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:13.080
become an increasingly crucial part of our
urgent care offering. At the moment, around

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:19.990
80% of the people who call in to 111 speak
to a call handler who is using digital technology

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:31.250
to advise them through a pathway and recommend
what they should do next. That means about

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:39.980
20% of them get to speak to a GP or a nursing
clinician for advice. We have committed to

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:45.899
increasing that proportion to 30%, and then
I expect it to rise to 40%, 50%, and to much

00:10:45.899 --> 00:10:55.500
higher numbers, because the evidence shows
that once you have used the technology to

00:10:55.500 --> 00:11:01.450
stream patients through a call handler, a
significant proportion then, if you actually

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:06.600
get to speak to a doctor, they are better
at judging risk and better at interpreting

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:10.380
the person who they are talking to than the
IT is.

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:16.820
We have to do two things simultaneously: one
is to get the technology enhanced so that,

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:20.940
as we deal with more patients, we learn from
it, we build the algorithms and we guide patients

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:25.560
to the right place as quickly as possible.
We are moving to make those algorithms available

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:29.690
online directly to patients, so that they
can answer the questions online and get advice,

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:35.100
and do not have to ring and speak to someone.
During next year, we will be enhancing those

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:43.029
algorithms with special protocols for children
and for people with mental health problems,

00:11:43.029 --> 00:11:52.310
and for the frail elderly, to make them more
specific. We also know that we should be diverting

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:56.330
more people to a telephone conversation with
a clinician, rather than telling them to go

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:02.480
to their GP or go to the A&amp;E department, and
that when we do that, more patients can have

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:07.510
their problems solved over the telephone.
We will also be investing in the technology

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:13.340
over the coming year to make it that, for
patients who grant permission, the doctor

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:17.240
they are speaking to on the phone can see
their records, and increasingly, would be

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:20.500
able to write them a prescription if that
were appropriate, and so on, so it becomes

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:21.899
a more holistic service.

00:12:21.899 --> 00:12:27.279
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: That is really good to hear.
Just to perhaps conclude that, do you have

00:12:27.279 --> 00:12:34.750
a date when you expect online self-assessment
to be, if you like, reinstated from the NHS

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:38.500
Direct service that was decommissioned with
the demise of NHS Direct?

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:43.800
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: There will be some data
published in the Delivery Plan, and I would

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.339
hate to spoil the excitement.

00:12:45.339 --> 00:12:46.924
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: Thank you; we look forward
to that.

00:12:46.924 --> 00:12:53.500
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: The answers will all be provided
tomorrow, as is always the way.

00:12:53.500 --> 00:13:06.110
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: This really follows on from
what Matthew has said, because both the operational

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:10.610
performance in the NHS and everything that
we are going to be publishing in the Delivery

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:17.390
Plan tomorrow is underpinned, of course, by
the work that has been going on in our national

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:24.740
priority programmes. They have continued to
make good progress across many areas, which

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:31.480
will be talked about in the Plan, but a lot
more work that clearly needs to be done, and

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:40.960
that is laid out in some granular detail in
the Delivery Plan. I will not go into detail

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:46.962
on those, but I did just want to say, as a
result of that thinking around the Delivery

00:13:46.962 --> 00:13:53.709
Plan, we have taken the opportunity to think
how those programmes need to be supported

00:13:53.709 --> 00:14:03.300
going forward so that they are more connected
to the local delivery in the STPs, through

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:11.810
the regions, and the programmes are being
realigned to that model more clearly and explicitly,

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:19.760
in many cases. Really, that is all I wanted
to say at this stage, unless there are any

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:38.560
particular questions on either the programme
status report or the corporate risks.

